-- Q9 [Synapsis, Tetrads & the Synaptonemal Complex | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub9$Synapsis, Tetrads & the Synaptonemal Complex$sub9$, $qt9$A meiocyte enters prophase I and assembles a normal synaptonemal complex along all of its chromosomes, but a mutation blocks every recombination nodule on chromosome 7 so that no chiasma ever forms on that homologous pair. When the synaptonemal complex later disassembles and the cell proceeds into anaphase I, what is the most likely consequence for chromosome 7?$qt9$, $op9$[{"label": "A", "text": "Its sister chromatids separate prematurely at anaphase I rather than waiting for anaphase II."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its two homologs are no longer physically tethered and may segregate improperly, causing nondisjunction."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It fails to complete S-phase replication and enters the tetrad with only two chromatids."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It cannot align at the metaphase plate because its spindle attachment site is lost."}]$op9$::jsonb, 'B', $ex9$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is B because, once the synaptonemal complex breaks down and the cohesin linking the homologous pair is removed, the only remaining physical connection holding the two homologs of a tetrad together is the chiasma. The text states that after the synaptonemal complex is gone "the pairs are held together only at the chiasmata" and that "There must be at least one chiasma per chromosome for proper separation of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I." With no chiasma on chromosome 7, its homologs are untethered as the cell reaches metaphase/anaphase I, so the spindle cannot reliably pull one homolog to each pole; the text identifies failure of homologous pairs to separate—often from "misaligned or incomplete synapsis"—as nondisjunction. (Choice A) is wrong because sister chromatids are held at the centromere by cohesin "until anaphase II," not by chiasmata, so they normally separate in meiosis II regardless of crossover. (Choice B) is correct because the chiasma is the sole tether remaining after synaptonemal-complex and cohesin-arm dissolution, and its absence permits improper homolog segregation (nondisjunction). (Choice C) is wrong because DNA replication occurs in S phase before prophase I and is independent of chiasma formation, so the chromosome still enters the tetrad with four chromatids. (Choice D) is wrong because spindle microtubules attach at the kinetochore on the centromere, not at the chiasma, so loss of a chiasma does not eliminate the attachment site needed for metaphase alignment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting a downstream segregation outcome at anaphase I from a specific molecular lesion by chaining the role of the chiasma as the last homolog tether to the definition of nondisjunction.$ex9$, 'hard', '1C', $cc9$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc9$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
