-- Q10 [Synapsis, Tetrads & the Synaptonemal Complex | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub10$Synapsis, Tetrads & the Synaptonemal Complex$sub10$, $qt10$In a human spermatocyte at prophase I, a cytologist observes that the X and Y chromosomes are joined by a synaptonemal complex along only a short terminal segment, whereas a chosen autosomal pair in the same cell is joined by a synaptonemal complex running its entire length. Which factor BEST accounts for why the X–Y complex is restricted to a short segment while the autosomal complex is complete?$qt10$, $op10$[{"label": "A", "text": "The X and Y differ so much in size that the spindle can attach only at their short shared arm, which caps how far the synaptonemal complex can extend."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The X and Y first synapse fully like the autosomes, but their synaptonemal complex disassembles earlier in prophase I, leaving only a short remnant still visible."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The synaptonemal complex assembles only where the chromosomes are homologous, and the X and Y share just a small region of homology, whereas the autosomes are homologous along their whole length."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Only the X and Y carry recombination nodules, and these nodules confine the synaptonemal complex to the homologous tip, whereas autosomes lack nodules and so synapse fully."}]$op10$::jsonb, 'C', $ex10$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is C because the synaptonemal complex forms between homologous chromosomes and can extend only where their sequences actually correspond, so the extent of pairing is set by how much of each chromosome is homologous. The text states that the complex "first forms at specific locations and then spreads outward to cover the entire length of the chromosomes," producing the tight pairing (synapsis) in which "the genes on the chromatids of the homologous chromosomes are aligned precisely with each other"; an autosomal pair is homologous along its whole length and therefore synapses completely. For the sex chromosomes, "even though the X and Y sex chromosomes are not completely homologous (that is, most of their genes differ), there is a small region of homology that allows the X and Y chromosomes to pair up during prophase I," and "a partial synaptonemal complex develops only between the regions of homology." Thus the short X–Y complex versus the full autosomal complex reflects the amount of shared homology, not chromosome size, timing of breakdown, or a special distribution of nodules. (Choice A) is incorrect because synaptonemal-complex formation depends on sequence homology rather than on size matching or spindle attachment, and the spindle microtubules engage kinetochores rather than dictating complex length. (Choice B) is incorrect because, although the synaptonemal complex does break down later in prophase I, the limitation here is in how far homology-based pairing can extend in the first place, not in differential disassembly—the X and Y never synapse fully. (Choice C) is correct because the complex assembles only across homologous regions, and the X and Y share only a small homologous region while autosomes are homologous throughout. (Choice D) is incorrect because recombination nodules are "located at intervals along the synaptonemal complex" of homologous pairs generally, marking future chiasmata on all synapsed chromosomes, and are not a sex-chromosome-only feature that caps complex length. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting a cytological observation (partial versus full pairing) and reasoning back to the underlying mechanism—homology-dependent assembly of the synaptonemal complex—rather than recalling a single stated fact.$ex10$, 'hard', '1C', $cc10$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc10$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
