UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a10B$Correctly notes the synaptonemal complex breaks down later in prophase I but wrongly makes differential disassembly, not limited homology, the cause of the short X–Y complex.$a10B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a10D$Invokes real recombination nodules but misassigns them exclusively to the sex chromosomes and as the structure that caps complex length.$a10D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Crossing Over & Recombination | easy | Skill 1 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub11$Crossing Over & Recombination$sub11$, $qt11$During prophase I of meiosis, a cytologist observes a tetrad and notes a point where genetic material has been physically swapped between two of the four chromatids, producing a chromatid that carries a new combination of maternal and paternal genes. Between which two structures does this exchange take place?$qt11$, $op11$[{"label": "A", "text": "Between the two sister chromatids of a single replicated chromosome"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Between chromatids of two non-homologous chromosomes carrying different genes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Between the spindle microtubules and the kinetochore of each chromosome"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Between nonsister chromatids of a pair of homologous chromosomes"}]$op11$::jsonb, 'D', $ex11$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is D because crossing over is defined as the reciprocal exchange of chromosomal segments between the nonsister chromatids of a synapsed pair of homologous chromosomes. The text states that "The synaptonemal complex supports the exchange of chromosomal segments between homologous nonsister chromatids—a process called crossing over," and that this "single crossover event between homologous nonsister chromatids leads to a reciprocal exchange of equivalent DNA," yielding a chromatid with a combination of maternal and paternal genes that did not exist before. The exchange is necessarily between the homologs (one maternal, one paternal) and not between identical sister copies, which is what generates the new gene combination the cytologist sees. (Choice A) Sister chromatids are identical copies of one chromosome, so an exchange between them would not create a new combination of maternal and paternal genes; this swaps "nonsister" for "sister." (Choice B) Crossing over requires synapsis of homologous chromosomes that carry the same genes; non-homologous chromosomes do not pair, so this names the wrong pairing partners. (Choice C) Microtubule-to-kinetochore attachment is the spindle-binding event of prometaphase I and moves chromosomes; it does not exchange DNA segments. (Choice D) This correctly identifies the exchange as occurring between nonsister chromatids of a homologous pair, the source of the recombinant chromatid. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the textbook definition of crossing over and the specific chromatids between which the reciprocal DNA exchange occurs.$ex11$, 'easy', '1C', $cc11$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc11$, 'biology', 1, 70
  ) RETURNING id
)
