UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a11B$Confusing which chromosomes pair, naming non-homologous chromosomes as the exchange partners$a11B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a11C$Substituting the prometaphase I spindle-kinetochore attachment for the prophase I DNA exchange$a11C$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Crossing Over & Recombination | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub12$Crossing Over & Recombination$sub12$, $qt12$A plant is heterozygous for two genes that lie close together on the same chromosome, having inherited the dominant alleles A and B on one homolog and the recessive alleles a and b on the other. During meiosis in some of its cells, a single crossover occurs in the chromosome segment between the two loci. A geneticist asks how this crossover changes the gametes produced by those cells relative to cells in which no crossover happens between the loci.$qt12$, $op12$[{"label": "A", "text": "The crossover generates gametes carrying nonparental allele combinations (Ab and aB) alongside the parental AB and ab types."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The crossover changes nothing, because genes on the same chromosome are always transmitted as the intact parental pair."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The crossover yields all four allele combinations (AB, Ab, aB, ab) in an exactly equal 1:1:1:1 ratio."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The crossover produces new combinations by rearranging the linear order of the two genes along the chromosome."}]$op12$::jsonb, 'A', $ex12$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is A because a crossover in the segment between two linked loci exchanges a chromosome segment between nonsister chromatids, placing alleles that started on opposite homologs (A with b, and a with B) onto the same chromatid. The text states that when a heterozygous AaBb individual is considered, "Because they correspond to new allele combinations, the genotypes Ab and aB are nonparental types that result from homologous recombination during meiosis. Parental types are progeny that exhibit the same allelic combination as their parents." Recombination thus produces some parental-type and some recombinant (nonparental) gametes, "the result of recombination is that maternal and paternal alleles are combined onto the same chromosome," while a recombinant chromatid "will carry a combination of maternal and paternal genes that did not exist before the crossover." (Choice A) is correct: the crossover unlinks A from B and a from b in some chromatids, creating the nonparental Ab and aB gametes in addition to the parental AB and ab. (Choice B) is a partial truth that holds only when no crossover occurs between the loci; the text notes that when "no crossing over occurs between them... the genes are always inherited together and all the offspring are the parental type," but the scenario specifies a crossover did happen. (Choice C) describes the unlinked outcome: the text says that "If the genes are unlinked, the individual should produce AB, Ab, aB, and ab gametes with equal frequencies, according to the Mendelian concept of independent assortment," whereas linked genes with occasional crossover yield recombinants as a minority, not a 1:1:1:1 ratio. (Choice D) misstates the mechanism, because "the genes are aligned during recombination, the gene order is not altered"; crossover swaps alleles between homologs without rearranging the linear order of the loci. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the mechanism of crossover to a specific dihybrid linkage scenario to predict how the gamete genotypes change relative to a no-crossover control.$ex12$, 'medium', '1C', $cc12$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc12$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
