UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a12C$Independent-assortment 1:1:1:1 outcome for genes on different chromosomes$a12C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a12D$Believes crossover rearranges gene order rather than swapping alleles$a12D$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Crossing Over & Recombination | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub13$Crossing Over & Recombination$sub13$, $qt13$A cytogeneticist tracks a single dividing germ cell and marks the exact moment when the synaptonemal complex zips two homologous chromosomes into tight alignment and recombination nodules cleave and rejoin nonsister chromatids, swapping a maternal segment for a paternal one. At which stage of meiosis is the cell captured at that moment?$qt13$, $op13$[{"label": "A", "text": "Metaphase I, as the paired homologs align randomly at the metaphase plate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Prophase I, while the synapsed homologs are held within the synaptonemal complex"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Anaphase I, as the separating homologs pull free and the chiasmata are broken"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Prophase II, as the haploid sets of sister chromatids recondense for the second division"}]$op13$::jsonb, 'B', $ex13$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is B because the synaptonemal complex forms and supports crossing over only during prophase I, and recombination nodules positioned along that complex mediate the cleavage and rejoining of nonsister chromatids at exactly the moment described. The text states that "The synaptonemal complex, a lattice of proteins between the homologous chromosomes, first forms" in prophase I and "supports the exchange of chromosomal segments between homologous nonsister chromatids—a process called crossing over." It further notes that "Located at intervals along the synaptonemal complex are large protein assemblies called recombination nodules," near which "the double-stranded DNA of each chromatid is cleaved, the cut ends are modified, and a new connection is made between the nonsister chromatids." The physical exchange itself therefore occurs while the homologs are synapsed in prophase I, even though the chiasma left behind persists until anaphase I. (Choice A) Metaphase I is when "the homologous chromosomes are arranged at the metaphase plate" and "oriented randomly," which drives independent assortment—a separate source of variation, not the moment of segment exchange. (Choice B) is correct because prophase I is the only stage in which the synaptonemal complex and its recombination nodules exist and actively carry out the exchange. (Choice C) Anaphase I is when "the chiasmata are broken... as the microtubules... pull the homologous chromosomes apart," meaning the leftover crossover junction is resolved here, but no DNA exchange takes place at this stage. (Choice D) Prophase II involves only recondensation of haploid sister-chromatid sets with new spindle formation; there is no synapsis of homologs and thus no crossing over. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because the student must map an observed molecular event (synaptonemal-complex-mediated cleavage and rejoining) onto the correct meiotic stage while distinguishing it from the later stage where its product, the chiasma, is merely resolved.$ex13$, 'medium', '1C', $cc13$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc13$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
