UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a16C$Citing a real prophase I event (synapsis/pairing) that organizes homologs rather than randomizing their orientation$a16C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a16D$Mistaking crossing over (recombination), a separate source of variation, for the basis of independent assortment$a16D$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Linkage & Independent Assortment | easy | Skill 1 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub17$Linkage & Independent Assortment$sub17$, $qt17$A geneticist studies a fruit fly in which the gene for body color and the gene for wing length sit very near each other on the same chromosome. She observes that the body-color and wing-length alleles a fly inherited from one parent almost always travel together into the same gamete, producing far more parental than recombinant offspring. What feature of these two genes best accounts for this pattern?$qt17$, $op17$[{"label": "A", "text": "They lie on separate non-homologous chromosomes, so they sort into gametes together purely by chance."}, {"label": "B", "text": "They lie physically close together on the same chromosome, so their parental alleles are usually transmitted into the same gamete."}, {"label": "C", "text": "They occupy the same locus on a pair of homologous chromosomes as alternative alleles of a single gene."}, {"label": "D", "text": "They lie far apart on the same chromosome, so frequent crossing over keeps their parental alleles together."}]$op17$::jsonb, 'B', $ex17$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is B because two genes lying in close proximity on the same chromosome are linked, and the text states that for such genes "their alleles tend to be transmitted through meiosis together," yielding mostly parental gametes. Because the loci are physically close, a crossover rarely falls between them, so the maternal alleles (or paternal alleles) the fly received stay coupled and move into the same gamete. This is exactly why linked genes violate the 9:3:3:1 expectation of independent assortment, producing an excess of parental over recombinant offspring. The closer the two genes, the lower the chance of a crossover separating them, reinforcing the parental pattern the geneticist observes. (Choice A) Genes on separate non-homologous chromosomes "will always sort independently," so they do not travel together as a unit; chance alignment cannot keep them coupled across many gametes. (Choice B) is correct because close physical proximity on a single chromosome is the defining cause of linkage, keeping parental allele combinations together. (Choice C) confuses linked genes with alleles of one gene; alleles occupy the same locus on homologs, but here we have two distinct genes at different loci, not alternative versions of one gene. (Choice D) reverses the distance relationship, since "as the distance between two genes increases, the probability of one or more crossovers between them increases," which separates alleles rather than keeping them together. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it asks the student to identify the defining structural cause of genetic linkage directly from established genetic principles.$ex17$, 'easy', '1C', $cc17$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc17$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
