-- Q18 [Linkage & Independent Assortment | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub18$Linkage & Independent Assortment$sub18$, $qt18$A geneticist test-crosses fruit flies and scores three genes on the same chromosome, finding a recombination frequency of 6% between genes M and N and 19% between genes M and P. Using Sturtevant's reasoning that recombination frequency reflects physical distance, which pair of genes lies farther apart on the chromosome?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "Genes M and N, because their lower recombination frequency means crossovers occur over a larger intervening region"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Genes M and N, because genes that recombine less frequently must lie on different chromosomes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Genes M and P, because their higher recombination frequency means a larger region lies between them where a crossover can occur"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Genes M and P, because a higher recombination frequency reflects how often their alleles are inherited together unchanged"}]$op18$::jsonb, 'C', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is C because the pair with the higher recombination frequency (M and P at 19%) is separated by the larger region of chromosome, since a bigger intervening stretch gives a crossover more opportunity to occur between them. The text states that Sturtevant "postulated that alleles that were far apart on a chromosome were more likely to dissociate during meiosis simply because there was a larger region over which recombination could occur," while "alleles that were close to each other on the chromosome were likely to be inherited together." Because recombination frequency "correlated with their genetic distance from each other," the 19% M-P frequency maps to a larger distance (about 19 cM) than the 6% M-N frequency (about 6 cM). The recombinant offspring counted to obtain these frequencies are the nonparental types produced by crossing over, not the parental combinations. (Choice A) is incorrect because it reverses the relationship: a lower recombination frequency indicates a smaller intervening region and closer genes, so M and N are the closer pair, not the farther one. (Choice B) is incorrect because a low recombination frequency indicates tight linkage on the same chromosome; only a frequency near 50% would make two genes behave as if they were on different chromosomes. (Choice C) is correct because the larger M-P region offers more chromosome length over which a crossover can occur, yielding the higher recombination frequency and the greater map distance. (Choice D) is incorrect because recombinants are the nonparental, crossed-over offspring; a higher recombination frequency reflects how often alleles are reshuffled, not how often they are inherited together unchanged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the principle that recombination frequency scales with physical distance to interpret given frequency data and rank the relative separation of two gene pairs.$ex18$, 'medium', '1C', $cc18$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
