UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a19B$Defaults to the 50 percent independent-assortment ceiling despite data showing few recombinants$a19B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a19C$Misconverts the decimal 0.08 to 0.8 percent instead of 8 percent$a19C$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Linkage & Independent Assortment | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub20$Linkage & Independent Assortment$sub20$, $qt20$A geneticist test-crosses fruit flies heterozygous for two genes (\(AaBb\)) to a homozygous recessive partner (\(aabb\)) and recovers 1,000 offspring: 910 match a parental allele combination and only 90 carry a nonparental combination. She is asked to predict what would happen to the nonparental proportion if she selected even more distantly spaced genes on that same chromosome. Which interpretation of these data, and of that prediction, is correct?$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "The two genes are linked but far enough apart for occasional crossovers, so the parental classes dominate while the nonparental fraction can climb toward but never exceed 50 percent."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The two genes lie on separate chromosomes and assort independently, which is why the nonparental offspring fall well below the much larger parental fraction."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The two genes are completely linked with no crossing over, so the small nonparental class must arise from a metaphase-I orientation error rather than from recombination."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The two genes are linked, and continued breeding of ever-more-distant genes could push the nonparental fraction above 50 percent because the genes are very far apart."}]$op20$::jsonb, 'A', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is A because a large parental excess with a small nonparental minority is the signature of two genes that are linked yet far enough apart for crossovers to happen occasionally, and the recombinant fraction is mathematically capped at 0.5. The text describes exactly this Morgan-type test cross (\(AaBb \times aabb\)) in which "both parental and nonparental cases occurred," with the parental classes far outnumbering the recombinants; a low recombination frequency "would indicate that the genes were definitively linked, but that they were far enough apart for crossovers to occasionally occur." Sturtevant showed recombination frequency can range "from linking perfectly (recombination frequency = 0) to unlinking perfectly (recombination frequency = 0.5)," and that the 0.5 value is the unlinked ceiling at which "50 percent of offspring are recombinants and the other 50 percent are parental types." Increasing distance raises recombination only up to—never beyond—that 50 percent ceiling, which is why the textbook asks whether a recombinant frequency could be 60 percent (it cannot). (Choice A) Correct: linked-but-separable genes give a parental majority, and the recombinant share approaches but cannot surpass the 0.5 unlinked maximum. (Choice B) Wrong: genes that assort independently produce all four gamete combinations "with equal frequencies," so parental and nonparental classes would be roughly equal, not the large parental excess observed here. (Choice C) Wrong: complete linkage with no crossing over yields zero recombinants and all parental offspring, so the existing nonparental minority is itself the product of crossing over, not a segregation error. (Choice D) Wrong: greater genetic distance does raise recombination, but only up to the 0.5 ceiling; no amount of further distance can drive the recombinant fraction above 50 percent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting test-cross count data to infer the linkage relationship and then reasoning about the mathematical limit that constrains a predicted recombinant frequency.$ex20$, 'hard', '1C', $cc20$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
