UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a20C$Attributes the recombinant minority to a metaphase-I orientation error under complete linkage, when complete linkage yields zero recombinants and the minority is the product of crossing over$a20C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a20D$Correctly notes distance increases recombination but wrongly concludes the fraction can exceed the 0.5 ceiling$a20D$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis | easy | Skill 1 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub21$Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis$sub21$, $qt21$During spermatogenesis in a male, the two members of one homologous chromosome pair fail to move to opposite poles at anaphase of meiosis I, so both end up in the same secondary spermatocyte. The resulting sperm carry an extra or a missing chromosome rather than the normal single copy. The event that produced these abnormal gametes is named:$qt21$, $op21$[{"label": "A", "text": "independent assortment, the random orientation of homologous tetrads at the metaphase plate that mixes maternal and paternal chromosomes into gametes"}, {"label": "B", "text": "nondisjunction, the failure of homologous chromosome pairs or sister chromatids to separate during meiosis, yielding gametes with an abnormal chromosome number"}, {"label": "C", "text": "crossing over, the reciprocal exchange of segments between homologous nonsister chromatids during prophase I that recombines linked alleles"}, {"label": "D", "text": "synapsis, the tight pairing of homologous chromosomes held together by the synaptonemal complex during prophase I"}]$op21$::jsonb, 'B', $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is B because the scenario describes a homologous pair that fails to separate at anaphase I, which is the defining event of nondisjunction. OpenStax states that chromosome number disorders "are caused by nondisjunction, which occurs when homologous chromosome pairs or sister chromatids fail to separate during meiosis," and that when "homologous chromosomes fail to separate during meiosis I, the result is two gametes that lack that particular chromosome and two gametes with two chromosome copies." This exactly matches gametes carrying an extra or missing chromosome, so the named event is nondisjunction. (Choice A) Independent assortment is the "random (or independent) assortment of homologous chromosomes at the metaphase plate"—a normal source of variation that still delivers one copy of each chromosome to each gamete, not an abnormal number. (Choice B) is correct because failure of homologs to separate is precisely the definition of nondisjunction and is what produces gametes with an aberrant chromosome count. (Choice C) Crossing over is "the exchange of chromosomal segments between homologous nonsister chromatids," which reshuffles alleles but does not change chromosome number. (Choice D) Synapsis is the "tight pairing of the homologous chromosomes" during prophase I; it precedes and is unrelated to the anaphase separation failure described. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the textbook definition of nondisjunction and distinguishing it from other named meiotic events.$ex21$, 'easy', '1C', $cc21$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a21A$Naming a different, normal meiotic variation mechanism (independent assortment) instead of the error process$a21A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a21C$Naming a different prophase-I variation mechanism (crossing over) that alters alleles but not chromosome number$a21C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a21D$Confusing the prophase-I pairing step (synapsis) with the anaphase separation failure$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub22$Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis$sub22$, $qt22$During spermatogenesis in a man, both homologous copies of chromosome 21 migrate together to the same pole during meiosis I, while every subsequent step of meiosis proceeds normally. A geneticist examines the four resulting sperm and tallies how many carry a normal single copy of chromosome 21.$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "Two sperm carry a normal single copy of chromosome 21, one carries two copies, and one lacks it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "All four sperm carry two copies of chromosome 21 because the unseparated pair is re-duplicated in meiosis II."}, {"label": "C", "text": "No sperm carries a normal single copy: two have two copies of chromosome 21 and two lack it entirely."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Three sperm carry a normal single copy of chromosome 21 and only one is aneuploid."}]$op22$::jsonb, 'C', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is C because when homologous chromosomes fail to separate in meiosis I, both members of the pair travel to one pole, so the two secondary cells are already abnormal and meiosis II simply splits each cell's chromatids without restoring balance. The text states that "If homologous chromosomes fail to separate during meiosis I, the result is two gametes that lack that particular chromosome and two gametes with two chromosome copies" (lines 16215-16217), meaning the cell receiving both homologs yields two two-copy gametes and the cell receiving neither yields two zero-copy gametes. Because all four gametes derive from these two unbalanced secondary cells, none ends up with the normal single copy. This contrasts with meiosis II nondisjunction, where only one of the two secondary cells is affected and two normal gametes still survive. (Choice A) Two normal, one with two copies, and one lacking the chromosome is the meiosis II outcome, where "the result is one gamete that lacks that chromosome, two normal gametes with one chromosome copy, and one gamete with two chromosome copies" (lines 16217-16219), not the meiosis I result described here. (Choice B) All four gametes cannot gain an extra copy, because "Interkinesis lacks an S phase, so chromosomes are not duplicated" (lines 13872-13873), so meiosis II cannot re-duplicate the chromosome. (Choice C) Correct: two gametes carry two copies and two lack the chromosome, so none is chromosomally normal. (Choice D) Three normal and one aneuploid misreads the event; "Nondisjunction can occur during either meiosis I or II, with differing results" (lines 16215-16216), and the meiosis I error makes all four gametes abnormal rather than sparing three. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires tracing the consequence of a single mechanistic error through two successive divisions to predict the chromosomal composition of all four final gametes.$ex22$, 'medium', '1C', $cc22$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
