UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a23B$Believes any division splitting chromosomes between two daughters lowers ploidy, so mitosis of a diploid cell is reductional$a23B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a23C$Confuses replication of chromatids (copy number per chromosome) with reduction of chromosome-set number (ploidy)$a23C$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub24$Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis$sub24$, $qt24$A geneticist analyzes the four haploid products of a single primary spermatocyte for one chromosome and finds two products carrying one copy of that chromosome, one product carrying two copies, and one product carrying zero copies. The geneticist concludes that a nondisjunction event occurred. At which stage did the error MOST likely take place?$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "Meiosis II, because sister chromatids failed to separate, producing one disomic, one nullisomic, and two normal products"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Meiosis I, because homologs failed to separate, producing the disomic and nullisomic products observed in the sample"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Prophase I, because a crossover between nonsister chromatids redistributed the chromosome unevenly among the products"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Mitosis in the spermatogonium, because an error before meiosis began made every resulting product aneuploid"}]$op24$::jsonb, 'A', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is A because a meiosis II error leaves two of the four gametes normal, which matches the observed 2 normal : 1 disomic : 1 nullisomic ratio. In a single spermatocyte, the two homologs separate normally at meiosis I, so each secondary spermatocyte is balanced; if sister chromatids then fail to separate in only one of those two cells during meiosis II, that cell yields one disomic and one nullisomic product while the other cell still divides correctly into two normal products. The text states that when sister chromatids fail to separate during meiosis II "the result is one gamete that lacks that chromosome, two normal gametes with one chromosome copy, and one gamete with two chromosome copies," exactly the pattern observed. The presence of any normal products rules out a meiosis I error, in which both homologs are missorted so that "two gametes that lack that particular chromosome and two gametes with two chromosome copies" result, leaving zero normal products. (Choice A) is correct: the 50 percent normal yield is the signature of sister-chromatid nondisjunction in meiosis II. (Choice B) is the 510-tempting partial truth: meiosis I nondisjunction also makes disomic and nullisomic gametes, but it produces zero normal products, contradicting the two normal products seen. (Choice C) is wrong because crossing over is "a reciprocal exchange of equivalent DNA between an egg-derived chromosome and a sperm-derived chromosome," exchanging segments rather than whole chromosomes, so it changes neither chromosome number nor count. (Choice D) reverses the logic: nondisjunction "occurs when homologous chromosome pairs or sister chromatids fail to separate during meiosis," and a pre-meiotic mitotic error in the spermatogonium would render all four products aneuploid, not leave two normal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring the meiotic stage of an error from a quantitative gamete-genotype ratio rather than recalling a definition.$ex24$, 'hard', '1C', $cc24$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
