-- Q25 [Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub25$Nondisjunction & Meiosis vs Mitosis$sub25$, $qt25$A researcher treats dividing germ-line cells with a drug that prevents assembly of the synaptonemal complex but leaves spindle formation, kinetochore function, and cohesin intact. The drug is applied to one population entering meiosis and to a second population of the same tissue undergoing ordinary mitosis. Which prediction best describes which divisions the drug disrupts?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "Meiosis I, meiosis II, and mitosis all fail, because homologous chromosomes must pair and synapse before any nuclear division can separate chromosomes."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both meiosis I and meiosis II fail, because the synaptonemal complex must reassemble in metaphase II to align the sister chromatids of each haploid cell."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Meiosis I proceeds but loses only crossing over, so homolog separation is normal and balanced haploid gametes are still produced."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Meiosis I fails while meiosis II and mitosis proceed normally, because synapsis and homolog pairing occur only in meiosis I."}]$op25$::jsonb, 'D', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)'. The answer is D because the synaptonemal complex, synapsis, and crossing over between homologs are events unique to meiosis I, so only meiosis I depends on the blocked step, while meiosis II and mitosis separate sister chromatids without pairing homologs. The text states that in meiosis I "the homologous chromosome pairs physically meet and are bound together with the synaptonemal complex... All of these events occur only in meiosis I" (13941-13946), so disabling synaptonemal-complex assembly specifically blocks meiosis I. The text also notes that "Meiosis II is analogous to a mitotic division" (13953) and recalls that "in mitosis, homologous chromosomes do not pair together" (13712-13713); both divisions therefore segregate sister chromatids without any synapsis to disrupt. Because chiasmata formed during synapsis hold homologs together, and "there must be at least one chiasma per chromosome for proper separation of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I" (13742-13743), blocking synapsis disrupts homolog segregation in meiosis I, not merely recombination. (Choice A) is wrong because homologous pairing is required only in meiosis I; mitosis explicitly does not pair homologs, so it cannot fail for lack of synapsis. (Choice B) is wrong because prometaphase II forms an individual kinetochore on each sister chromatid that attaches to opposite poles, with no synaptonemal complex involved, so meiosis II is unaffected. (Choice C) is a tempting partial truth but is wrong because losing synapsis also eliminates the chiasmata needed for proper homolog separation, so segregation in meiosis I fails rather than proceeding to balanced gametes. (Choice D) is correct because only meiosis I requires synaptonemal-complex-mediated pairing, leaving the sister-chromatid-separating divisions of meiosis II and mitosis intact. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the meiosis-versus-mitosis contrast to predict the differential effect of a targeted molecular perturbation across three distinct divisions.$ex25$, 'hard', '1C', $cc25$Meiosis & Genetic Variability (BIO)$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
